Yes. The new cache key is too narrow, and that makes the cache unsound.

**The bug:** the new `ComputeEntry` hashes only the nonce, the range proof, and the value commitment. It drops the asset commitment and the scriptPubKey. Both of those are inputs to the verification the cache is standing in for. In Elements, `secp256k1_rangeproof_verify` takes the scriptPubKey as the extra commitment data and takes the generator derived from the asset commitment. A proof is only valid for one specific asset generator and one specific script.

**Why that matters:** once a (proof, value commitment) pair has been verified and stored, any later output that reuses the same proof and value commitment will hit the cache and be reported valid, even if its asset commitment or scriptPubKey is different. Real verification with those parameters would fail. An attacker can get a legitimate output cached by relaying a valid transaction, then submit a second transaction that reuses the proof bytes with a different asset commitment or destination script. The node skips verification and accepts it. That is a consensus and inflation bug, not just a performance regression.

**The fix** is to keep the asset commitment and the scriptPubKey in the hash:

```cpp
void ComputeEntry(uint256& entry, const std::vector<unsigned char>& proof,
                  const std::vector<unsigned char>& valueCommitment,
                  const std::vector<unsigned char>& assetCommitment,
                  const CScript& scriptPubKey)
{
    CSHA256().Write(nonce.begin(), nonce.size())
             .Write(proof.data(), proof.size())
             .Write(valueCommitment.data(), valueCommitment.size())
             .Write(assetCommitment.data(), assetCommitment.size())
             .Write(scriptPubKey.data(), scriptPubKey.size())
             .Finalize(entry.begin());
}
```

Since the three vectors are variable length, consider writing a length prefix before each one so that different splits of the same concatenated bytes cannot collide.

**One thing the diff does fix:** the old code wrapped the value commitment in `CPubKey`. A commitment starts with 0x08 or 0x09, which `CPubKey::GetLen` does not recognize, so the object was invalidated and contributed zero bytes to the hash. The old key never actually included the value commitment. The new code includes it correctly. Just do not throw away the other two bindings in the process.
